From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Sun, 14 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AUTO_MEMORIES: only convert a memory that is its own module

A memory inferred inside a larger module was being converted. The macro
was generated, the module name written into blackboxes.txt, and
`blackbox <name>` then matched nothing -- because the name is a memory
inside a module, not a module. The design synthesized to flip-flops
while memories.json reported the memory as converted.

That is worse than not converting it: the flow completes, the inventory
and the netlist disagree, and the area and power look plausible. On
picorv32 the register file -- an inline `reg [31:0] cpuregs [0:31]`
array -- was recorded as

    "behavioral_model": {"module": "$paramod$...\\picorv32"},
    "idiomatic": true, "reason": "meets ASAP7 macro floors"

and synthesized as 1024 flip-flops. Identical on the partition and the
serial synthesis paths.

An inline array is the design asking for flip-flops, so leaving it alone
is the right answer and the reason says how to get the other one:
instantiate the memory as its own module. The gate is whether the memory
is its own behavioral model, which is what "memories at the module
boundary" already means everywhere else in this code.

Not upstreamed. Retire at a bump onto an ORFS that carries the check.
---
 flow/scripts/memories/idiomatic.py | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/flow/scripts/memories/idiomatic.py b/flow/scripts/memories/idiomatic.py
--- a/flow/scripts/memories/idiomatic.py
+++ b/flow/scripts/memories/idiomatic.py
@@ -23,8 +23,38 @@
 MAX_TOTAL_PORTS = 4
 
 
+def enclosing_module(mem: schema.Memory) -> str | None:
+    """The module whose body simulates this memory, demangled.
+
+    yosys writes a parameterized module as "$paramod$<hash>\\<name>" or
+    "$paramod\\<name>\\<param>=<value>"; the module is the component after
+    the first backslash in both forms.
+    """
+    model = mem.behavioral_model or {}
+    module = model.get("module")
+    if not module:
+        return None
+    if module.startswith("$paramod"):
+        parts = module.split("\\")
+        return parts[1] if len(parts) > 1 else None
+    return module.lstrip("\\")
+
+
 def judge(mem: schema.Memory) -> tuple[bool, str]:
     """Return (idiomatic, reason)."""
+    # Only a memory that *is* a module can be converted: conversion works
+    # by blackboxing the module so the generated liberty view replaces its
+    # behavioral body. A memory inferred inside a larger module has no
+    # module to blackbox, so converting it would generate a macro nothing
+    # instantiates and report a conversion that did not happen.
+    enclosing = enclosing_module(mem)
+    if enclosing is not None and enclosing != mem.name:
+        return (
+            False,
+            f"inferred inside module {enclosing} rather than being one; "
+            "an inline array is the design asking for flip-flops. "
+            "Instantiate the memory as its own module to convert it",
+        )
     if mem.bits < 1:
         return False, "no data pins"
     if mem.rows < MIN_ROWS:
